WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:03.560
What is a shawl collar?

2
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.960
If you go to the dictionary,
by definition, it's a turned over collar

3
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:15.080
of a garment that combines with lapels
forming an unbroken curving line.

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.520
Now as knitters, we usually don't think

5
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:22.800
in terms of lapels, but we do
recognize the turned over collar part.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.480
There are a lot of variations of this
style of sweater,

7
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:31.440
but I think we can agree that it's
a sweater, a pullover or cardigan

8
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:37.320
with an extended band that folds back,
forming a collar.

9
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.840
Let's look at them from a machine
knitter's point of view.

10
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.440
First, let's look at cardigans.

11
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.840
This is a shawl collar cardigan,
it's just a wide band running from the hem

12
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.240
up the front, around the neck, back
and down the other side to the other hem.

13
00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.960
You could use any V-neck cardigan pattern

14
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.920
to knit this. Probably one
with a deeper V would work best.

15
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.560
There are some considerations. Since

16
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.720
the band folds over,
we need to knit a reversible fabric like

17
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:21.960
ribbing or garter stitch,
or we could knit a double layer band.

18
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:28.040
We can knit the band/collar
combination in two ways.

19
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.040
The first way would be a long,

20
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:37.120
skinny band knit in one piece going all
the way around the edge of the garment.

21
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.160
But traditionally, shawl collars are knit

22
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.760
in this direction, working
from the garment edge out.

23
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.200
As machine knitters we have
our needle bed limitation.

24
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:55.880
But we could knit the band in pieces
and then seam the pieces together.

25
00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.080
Depending on the length of your sweater,
you might be able to strategically

26
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:06.360
position the seams at the center
back neck or the shoulder seams.

27
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.200
Or we could always just pick up
that edge and knit the band by hand.

28
00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:21.080
A common variation of a shawl collar
cardigan includes a shaped cardigan band.

29
00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.200
Now, if you're knitting that skinny band
lengthwise, you could shape as you're

30
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.960
knitting and increasewidth-wise
stitches to form the collar.

31
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:38.640
If you're knitting width-wise, you could
use short-row to form that collar.

32
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.440
So a shawl collar cardigan is pretty easy

33
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:47.520
to create without any changes
to a basic V-neck pattern.

34
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.280
Pull over shawl collars are very popular.

35
00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.960
Again, it's just a band,
straight or shaped,

36
00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:02.000
knit in a reversible pattern so that it
can fold over to form the collar.

37
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.720
The collar and band combination is set

38
00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:09.680
into a V neck opening,
usually with a squared bottom edge.

39
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.280
There are many, many variations on this
design.

40
00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.600
it's popular for small children because
the neck opening is large enough to go

41
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:23.360
over the head, but the collar comes
up close to the neck for warmth.

42
00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.800
The secret to crafting a shawl collar

43
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.960
pullover is to make the garment neck
opening wide enough to accommodate

44
00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:36.600
the collar and to make the neck depth
deep enough so the collar can fold over.

45
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.040
Looking through your machine knitter's
eyes, start examining shawl collar

46
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:45.040
sweaters and ready-to-wear
and hand knitting patterns.

47
00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.320
You'll be amazed at how many different

48
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:53.440
versions you'll find based on a simple
band that folds over to form a collar.

